course: adult-life
lesson: 51
title: Scary news: wars, disasters and how to talk about them
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The news on TV, the radio and phones shows real things: wars, earthquakes, floods, fires and accidents. It shows the worst moments, because what is new and unusual today is what makes the news.
explain: Scary news is real, but it is usually far away, it is usually rare, and the same pictures are shown again and again, so the world can feel much worse than it is.
explain: Vivid example: in a kitchen in Manchester the six o'clock news shows smoke over a city and the word "missiles". Ollie asks if he should sleep with his trainers on. Mum turns the sound down, finds the country on the map with him and says, "That's thousands of miles away, love. Nobody is attacking us."
explain: Good grown-ups do four things with scary news: they turn it down, they tell the truth simply, they show the helpers, and they find one small thing the family can do. Asking questions and asking for a hug are always allowed.
fact: Wars, disasters and accidents on the news are real, but most happen far away; finding the place on a map shrinks the fear to its proper size.
fact: One event is often shown many times on different channels and phones; ten showings of a bombed building are still one building.
fact: When thousands of people flee a war, they are called refugees; they stay in camps, schools and other people's homes, and charities help them.
fact: Big earthquakes, volcanoes and hurricanes happen in particular parts of the world; where you live the chance can be very small, and buildings in earthquake places are built specially.
fact: A hurricane on a weather map is a forecast of where a storm may go in the next days; warnings give people time to get ready or leave.
fact: Plane crashes and terror attacks are on the news because they are very rare; millions of flights land safely every day.
fact: A missing child on the news is a rare case; police use the news so that the public can help, and the family safety rules still work.
fact: Wars can end: peace treaties have been signed, and countries that once fought each other are friends now.
fact: Children are never sent to fight in a war in countries like ours; a playground claim that "we'll all be called up" is a rumour.
fact: Newsreaders speak slowly and look serious about deaths to show respect for the people who died and their families.
fact: You do not have to feel sad about a famous person you never knew; the people laying flowers knew their songs or films.
fact: Checking scary news again and again, especially late at night, keeps the worry going and makes nobody safer.
fact: It is all right to be happy, have a party or laugh while sad things happen far away; you can be kind and joyful at once.
fact: "The world has gone mad" is a grown-up grumbling, not a fact; most people in the world are having ordinary, peaceful days.
exercise: The news shows "missiles" over a city far away. Ollie asks if they could reach his house tonight. What helps most? | Finding the country on a map and seeing how far away it is | Packing a bag and sleeping with his trainers on, just in case | Watching the news all night so he is ready if missiles come | Not asking, because grown-ups get cross
exercise: Ava sees the same bombed building on TV, on Dad's phone and on a screen in a shop. How many buildings were bombed? | One, shown many times | Three, one for each screen | A new one every single hour | Nobody can know at all
exercise: A reporter says "thousands have fled their homes". Where do the children sleep now? | In camps, schools and other people's homes | Out in the open every single night | Back home, because fleeing is pretend | In hotels paid for by the newsreader
exercise: Mia sees an earthquake on the news and asks if the ground under her school could open up tomorrow. What is true? | Big earthquakes happen in certain places, and hers is not one | Earthquakes happen everywhere in the world equally often | Every earthquake on TV moves on to the next country after it | The ground can open under any school at any time
exercise: It's raining hard on Jake's street the day the news shows floods with cars floating. What is true? | A rainy day is not a flood; the flood is by a burst river elsewhere | His house will be under water by morning, like the cars on TV | All rain turns into a flood within a day | Floods only happen in the news on TV, never in real life to real people
exercise: Isla cries for a koala carried out of a fire by a firefighter. What is the part of the story she might miss? | Firefighters and vets are rescuing many animals | All the koalas in the whole world have died in the fire | The fire was started on purpose by the koalas | The firefighter is an actor in a film
exercise: Noah hears "terror attack" on the car radio and is scared of the football ground on Saturday. What does Dad say that is true? | Attacks are very rare, and police and security work every day | Attacks happen at every big match | It is wrong to ask questions about the news | The radio was making it all up for fun
exercise: Why did the newsreader look sad when she talked about a plane crash? | To show respect for the people who died | Because she was on the plane herself that morning | Because newsreaders are told to cry every evening | Because planes crash somewhere every single day
exercise: Harry asks if wars ever end. What does Grandad tell him? | Yes; many wars ended in peace, and old enemies are friends now | No; once a war starts it can never stop, it just goes on for ever | Only if one of the two countries disappears completely | Only when children vote to stop it
exercise: Freya sees children her age queuing for food and is angry. What is a good next step? | Give a little to a charity that sends food | Stop eating her own tea so that things are fair | Turn the anger into shouting at Mum and Dad | Decide that nothing can ever be done
exercise: Charlie hears his parents talking quietly late at night and lies awake. What should he do? | Ask in the morning; it was about the news, not about them | Guess the worst and keep it to himself | Listen at the door every night from now on | Decide the family is in terrible trouble
exercise: Grace sees a missing child on the news and is scared to walk to the corner shop. What does Mum do? | Explains it is rare and goes over the family safety rules | Says strangers are everywhere, so stay indoors | Tells her it is a made-up story to scare kids | Says missing children are on the news every day
exercise: The teacher talked about the war; Mum and Dad said "don't worry about it". Is someone lying? | No; the teacher gave facts and the parents gave comfort | Yes, the teacher made the war up to frighten the class | Yes, the parents are hiding a big secret about the war from her | No, because the war has already ended
exercise: A classmate says "war's coming here next week and we'll all get called up as soldiers". True? | No; it's a playground rumour, and children never go to war | Yes; every child gets a soldier's uniform at ten years old | Yes, because the news said "war" this week, so it is coming here next | Only for children who are good at running
exercise: Max sees lava on the news and thinks a volcano could pop up in his garden. What helps? | A map showing where volcanoes are and why | Digging up the garden to check for lava underneath | Watching more volcano news every night | Moving house to somewhere colder
exercise: Lily's party is the day after a sad war story. Should they cancel it? | No; it's fine to have the party and be kind too | Yes; nobody may have fun anywhere while there is a war | Yes; parties make wars last longer | No, but she must not smile at it
exercise: Oscar watches the news on his tablet late at night to see if the war has stopped. What is best? | Leave the tablet out of the bedroom; Mum will tell him news | Keep checking all night, or something bad will happen to the family | Watch even more, so he knows everything | Sleep with the tablet under his pillow
exercise: After a week of bad news Evie asks, "Are we going to be all right?" What does she need most? | A hug, some good news, and a break from the news | A long list of every war in the world, with all the details | To watch the news on her own for longer | To be told she is too young to ask
test: Little brother, eyes wide: "they showed the bomb thing AGAIN, that's like the fourth one today." Put him right. | It's one event, shown again and again | Four separate bombs have gone off today | The bombs are getting closer each time | A new one happens every time it's shown
test: Gran's on the phone: "...and the poor little ones, sleeping in a school hall, it breaks my heart." Ollie can't eat his tea. What does Gran say next that helps? | Feeling sorry shows a kind heart; we'll give to Friday's collection | Eat up your tea now, because those children far away don't really matter | Stop feeling anything about it at once | Hide your tea so nobody feels guilty
test: Your mate: "Plane crashed, it's all over the telly, I'm NEVER flying to Spain lol." What's the honest answer? | Crashes are news because they almost never happen | Planes crash every single day but the airlines hide it | He's right; flying is the most dangerous thing | Only planes to Spain ever crash
test: Grandad, at the telly: "The world's gone bloody mad." Finn won't go out to play. What did Grandad mean? | He was grumbling; most people are having ordinary, peaceful days | The world really has gone mad since this morning, and nobody knows why yet | It is dangerous to go outside today | Grandad has found out something secret
test: Sophie: "the swirly storm thing's smashing the town right now!!" The TV says it will reach the coast on Thursday. What's true? | It's a forecast; the warning gives people time to get ready | The town has already been destroyed | Storms on maps never come at all | It is happening in the TV studio
test: Mum to Dad, low voice: "...the boy's still missing, police want any sightings." Grace hears and won't go to the shop. What is the sensible view? | It's rare; the news asks the public to help; keep our safety rules | Children go missing on every street every day, so no shop is safe any more | She must never go anywhere ever again | It's not true; the police just like being on TV
test: Big sister, scrolling at midnight: "just checking if the war's over, then I'll sleep, promise." Why is this a bad idea? | Checking again and again keeps the worry going and makes nobody safer | The war will only end if someone watches | Phones explode if used after midnight | She'll miss the end if she sleeps now
test: Teacher in class: "You might have heard about the war. Here's a map, and here are the facts." At home Dad says, "Don't you worry." Which is right? | Both; facts and comfort fit together | Only the teacher; Dad doesn't really know | Only Dad; the teacher shouldn't talk about it | Neither; nobody should ever mention wars
test: Kid at school: "My cousin says wars never end, like EVER, they just go on." Put him right. | Many wars have ended, and peace is work people do every day | He's right; no war in the whole of history has ever really ended | Wars end only when one side is all gone | Wars end only when the news stops filming
test: Lily: "Is it wrong to have my party when people are sad far away?" Which answer is true? | No; you can be happy and still care, like putting a coin in the charity box | Yes; fun is banned until every war is over | Yes; having a party while others are sad will make things worse over there | No, because faraway people don't matter
test: Theo's friend: "You didn't cry when that actor died?? that's so heartless." Is Theo heartless? | No; it's normal not to feel sad about someone you never knew | Yes; everyone must grieve for every famous person, whether they knew them or not | Yes; he should pretend to cry tomorrow | No, because the actor didn't really die
exam: Identify the principal reason for which disasters and acts of violence feature prominently in news broadcasts. | They are unusual and recent, and therefore newsworthy | They occur in every locality on a daily basis without exception | Broadcasters are obliged to show only misfortune | They are invented in order to attract viewers
exam: Whereas a single incident is broadcast repeatedly across several channels and devices, determine the number of incidents which have in fact occurred. | One incident, presented on multiple occasions | As many incidents as there were broadcasts, channels and devices | A number which increases with each hour | A number which no person can ascertain
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the obligations of children in the event of armed conflict in countries such as the United Kingdom. | Children are not called upon to serve as soldiers | All children above the age of nine are conscripted for military service | Children are conscripted upon the declaration of war | Children may be conscripted if a rumour so states
exam: Ascertain the course of conduct most conducive to the wellbeing of a child who has been distressed by repeated reports of war. | A respite from the news, together with reassurance and comfort | The continuous monitoring of news reports by the child throughout the night | The concealment of his distress from his parents | The cancellation of all enjoyable activities indefinitely
